+# Decision: Transport for delivering committed changes to browsers
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard keeps one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS and the server assigns each committed edit one event revision; browsers display a revisioned view. What remains open is the transport that carries committed revisions from the server to up to 800 simultaneous browsers per event, at up to 20 edits per second during opening-time bursts, with recovery after a browser has been disconnected for up to 20 minutes.
+
+The transport is the hinge for three unwritten design nodes — the change store, delivery, and browser sync — whose shape depends on it: whether the store serves cursor reads or a replayable publication stream, whether delivery answers requests or manages long-lived sessions, and whether browsers poll, subscribe, or reconnect-and-replay. Getting it wrong is expensive because all three nodes are sized around it; revisiting the transport later means reworking the store's read model, the delivery layer, and the browser's recovery logic together.
+
+Every candidate below meets the standing board invariants, so these are background, not differentiators: one authoritative revision per committed edit regardless of route; the 15-second freshness target under normal connectivity; 800 concurrent browsers with 20-edit/second bursts; and recovery of the latest board after up to a 20-minute disconnect. The project's tenets permit both self-hosted and managed infrastructure, so a hosted option is eligible on equal footing — provider coupling is a cost to weigh, not a disqualifier.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+No gate eliminates any candidate: all three meet the freshness, scale, and recovery targets. Selection turns entirely on weighted criteria.
+
+- **Vendor independence** — *major*. Whether the board can run without a contract with, or a dependency on, an external realtime provider. Weighted major for this decision: the board is operational infrastructure for weekend markets, and the project prefers not to tie its core update path to a provider's pricing or availability.
+- **Update latency** — *moderate*. How quickly a committed revision reaches connected browsers. The 15-second target is a floor every option clears; subsecond updates "would be useful but are not required," so latency below the floor is a genuine but secondary good.
+- **Connection-state visibility** — *moderate*. How readily the system can tell a browser that its view may be stale rather than letting a quiet board be mistaken for an unchanged one. Turns on the tenet *Connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board.*
+- **Recurring operating cost** — *moderate*. Ongoing spend to run the transport at the target scale. Neither cost nor operational burden is declared the overriding criterion, so this is weighed, not decisive.
+- **Operational burden** — *moderate*. Engineering and run-time ownership the team takes on: connection capacity, reconnection handling, and any replay machinery.
+- **Connectivity robustness across restrictive networks** — *moderate*. How reliably the transport survives mobile, venue wifi, and proxied networks that coordinators use on-site. Cuts against long-lived connections, which some networks drop or block more aggressively than plain request/response.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** Browsers re-request the board every five seconds over ordinary HTTPS, passing a cursor (the last revision they hold); the server returns any revisions committed since. Delivery stays request/response with no session state. The change store needs cursor-indexed reads so a poll can answer "everything after revision N" cheaply.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Vendor independence: **strong** — runs on the same HTTPS stack as commands, no external provider.
+- Update latency: **weak** — a committed change surfaces only on the next poll, so up to ~five seconds of lag; within the freshness target but the furthest from subsecond.
+- Connection-state visibility: **partial** — a failed poll is observable, but between polls a browser cannot distinguish "nothing changed" from "not reaching the server" without added signaling.
+- Recurring operating cost: **weak** — 800 browsers polling every five seconds is sustained repeated-read load even when the board is idle.
+- Operational burden: **strong** — stateless request/response, no connections or reconnection logic to own.
+- Connectivity robustness: **strong** — plain short-lived HTTPS traverses restrictive networks and proxies most reliably.
+
+**Summary.** Lowest operational burden and most robust across networks, paid for in update lag and steady read load.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description.** The server holds a live WebSocket to each browser and pushes new revisions as they commit. The change store keeps replayable revisions and a publication mechanism that reliably hands each committed revision to the delivery layer. Delivery manages the socket sessions and fans revisions out to connected browsers. Browsers hold a subscription and, after a drop, reconnect and catch up from the last revision they held.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Vendor independence: **strong** — the socket layer is hosted by the project; no realtime provider in the path.
+- Update latency: **strong** — revisions are pushed as they commit, well below the freshness floor.
+- Connection-state visibility: **strong** — a live connection makes a drop directly observable, so a stale view can be flagged rather than mistaken for an unchanged board.
+- Recurring operating cost: **partial** — no provider fees, but connection capacity for 800 concurrent browsers plus bursts is a standing server cost to provision.
+- Operational burden: **weak** — the project owns connection-capacity management, reconnection handling, and the replay path for catch-up; the heaviest ownership of the three.
+- Connectivity robustness: **partial** — long-lived sockets are dropped or blocked by some venue and mobile networks more often than short HTTPS requests, placing more weight on reconnect/catch-up.
+
+**Summary.** Low-latency pushed updates with no vendor in the path, paid for in connection and reconnection ownership.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** A third-party realtime provider carries the fan-out. The server publishes committed revisions to the provider; browsers subscribe through the provider's client using its subscription model. The change store needs publication reconciliation so the provider's view and the authoritative revision history cannot diverge; delivery wraps the provider and holds its credentials.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Vendor independence: **weak** — the core update path depends on a provider's pricing, availability, and client library.
+- Update latency: **strong** — providers are built for low-latency fan-out, below the freshness floor.
+- Connection-state visibility: **strong** — provider clients expose connection and subscription state the browser can act on.
+- Recurring operating cost: **weak** — recurring per-connection or per-message fees at 800 concurrent browsers plus bursts.
+- Operational burden: **strong** — the provider owns connection capacity and reconnection; the project owns only publication reconciliation and credentials.
+- Connectivity robustness: **strong** — mature provider clients handle fallback transports and reconnection across restrictive networks.
+
+**Summary.** Quickest route to low-latency updates with the least connection ops, paid for in recurring fees and provider coupling.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.**
+
+The decision turns on the major criterion, **vendor independence**, where self-hosted sockets and polling are both strong and the hosted service is weak. Between the two independent options, the pushed socket model wins the moderate criteria that matter to a live coordination board: **update latency** (strong vs. polling's weak five-second lag) and **connection-state visibility** (strong vs. polling's partial), the latter turning on the tenet *Connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board* — a live socket makes a drop directly observable, where polling must add signaling to tell staleness from silence. The hosted service matches sockets on latency and visibility but is set aside because its strengths are bought with the recurring fees and provider coupling this decision weighs against; its low operational burden does not outweigh the major independence criterion, and neither cost nor burden is the overriding criterion here.
+
+This choice is taken with eyes open on the criteria it loses. **Operational burden** is weak: the project accepts owning connection capacity for 800-plus concurrent browsers, reconnection handling, and the replay path for catch-up. **Connectivity robustness** is only partial: long-lived sockets fare worse than short HTTPS on some venue and mobile networks, which raises the stakes on reconnect-and-catch-up working correctly. These are the accepted price of low-latency updates with no vendor in the path.
+
+Committing forecloses leaning on a managed layer for fan-out or reconnection, and obligates the three downstream nodes: the **change store** holds replayable revisions and a publication outbox that reliably hands each committed revision to delivery, and serves revision-replay for catch-up; **delivery** owns the WebSocket sessions and fan-out; and **browser sync** holds a subscription and, after a disconnect, reconnects and catches up from its last held revision. The visibility tenet becomes a concrete obligation on browser sync: a dropped or stalled connection must surface to the user, not pass as an unchanged board.
