 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Committed changes delivered over self-hosted WebSockets.** The server pushes each committed revision to connected browsers over a WebSocket it hosts itself. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected for the repeated read load and the few seconds of update lag the pushed model avoids; a hosted realtime service was rejected for its recurring fees and coupling to a provider. Self-hosted sockets accept the operational cost of owning connection capacity and reconnection handling in exchange for low-latency updates with no vendor coupling. See `docs/decisions/delivery-transport.md`.
